### Capacity

- Knee: between 180.8/s offered, the highest plateau that qualifies with every one below it (achieved 180.7/s), and 210.9/s, the first plateau that fails.
- Peak: 246.0/s achieved, in scout window 16, whatever its latency.
- Population: 241,024 to 1,390,524 people at their peak, at 13 to 75 peak arrivals/s per 100,000, read only at corpus scale S (100,000 EHRs) on example 8-vCPU VM, 8 cores, 8 GiB, NVMe storage, single node.
- Scalability fit (an explanatory model, never a verdict): α 0.3526, β 0.000000, R² 0.975 over 21 points; no finite maximum.

The population band rests on: About 46 clinical documents per capita per year (consultations, discharges and ED visits from OECD Health at a Glance 2023 and Eurostat; laboratory results from RCPath; imaging from the NHS Diagnostic Imaging Dataset 2023/24; prescription items from NHS BSA PCA 2024/25) give 0.15 writes per second per 100,000 population; a peak factor of 8 (the ITU-T E.500 busy hour) and a read multiplier of 10:1 to 50:1 (OLTP-Bench and YCSB; PMC10148376) give 13 to 75 peak API arrivals per second per 100,000. The knee divided by that band is the population a deployment of this shape could serve at its peak, read only at the corpus scale and on the environment the run used.

p99 by offered rate over the scout windows and the plateaus, 8.9/s to 247.3/s, on a log scale, `·` where p99 does not resolve:

